1
00:00:00,142 --> 00:00:06,948
Imagine designing a molecule on a computer, making it in the laboratory, and watching it block a deadly snake toxin.

2
00:00:07,438 --> 00:00:09,840
Scientists have done that with help from AI.

3
00:00:10,062 --> 00:00:16,068
Other teams have used it to improve plastic degrading enzymes and design new tools for editing DNA.

4
00:00:16,270 --> 00:00:25,280
These results point toward a future where scientists can design more of the biological machinery we need for medicine, food, and the environment.

5
00:00:25,614 --> 00:00:32,880
Here are seven ways AI is improving biology, what researchers have actually achieved, and where this could lead.

6
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:35,780
First, that snake toxin experiment.

7
00:00:36,302 --> 00:00:42,084
Some snake toxins cause paralysis by interfering with the signals that let nerves control muscles.

8
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:48,720
Researchers wanted to design proteins that could grab those toxins before they attached to their targets.

9
00:00:49,006 --> 00:00:52,132
Proteins are tiny pieces of biological machinery.

10
00:00:52,398 --> 00:01:00,240
Their shape helps determine what they interact with, so designing one that grips a particular toxin is a demanding molecular puzzle.

11
00:01:00,462 --> 00:01:06,340
A team involving the University of Washington used AI to propose new protein designs.

12
00:01:06,766 --> 00:01:09,920
Scientists then made them and measured whether they worked.

13
00:01:10,254 --> 00:01:17,732
In selected experiments, the design proteins protected every mouse tested against particular purified neurotoxins.

14
00:01:18,158 --> 00:01:25,476
Those were small groups of five, but they demonstrated that a computer-generated design could become a working antitoxin.

15
00:01:25,742 --> 00:01:26,880
There are major steps ahead.

16
00:01:27,342 --> 00:01:34,052
Snake venom contains many components, and a separate experiment did not show significant protection against whole venom skin damage.

17
00:01:34,382 --> 00:01:37,028
Human safety and effectiveness still need testing.

18
00:01:37,262 --> 00:01:40,240
What makes this exciting is the design capability.

19
00:01:40,526 --> 00:01:48,960
Scientists can ask for a protein with a useful job, use AI to propose candidates, and then find out whether those candidates perform that job.

20
00:01:49,262 --> 00:01:52,960
That same idea also has applications in waste.

21
00:01:53,454 --> 00:01:57,380
An enzyme is a biological tool that speeds up a chemical reaction.

22
00:01:57,710 --> 00:02:04,880
Some enzymes can break apart certain plastics, but making them effective enough for useful recycling is an engineering challenge.

23
00:02:05,166 --> 00:02:15,908
Researchers at UT Austin used machine learning to help improve an existing enzyme that breaks down PET, the plastic used in many bottles and food containers.

24
00:02:16,238 --> 00:02:19,332
The model helped identify promising changes to the protein.

25
00:02:19,630 --> 00:02:23,172
Scientists built and tested the resulting versions.

26
00:02:23,470 --> 00:02:34,500
Their enzyme, called Fast PET ACE, almost completely broke down PET samples from 51 used products within a week under laboratory conditions.

27
00:02:34,830 --> 00:02:39,360
They also recovered the chemical ingredients and used them to make PET again.

28
00:02:39,662 --> 00:02:41,200
That is a compelling possibility.

29
00:02:41,550 --> 00:02:45,316
Biological machinery helping recover useful material from waste.

30
00:02:45,678 --> 00:02:51,972
The challenge now includes handling tougher material and making the process work economically at scale.

31
00:02:52,270 --> 00:02:56,452
Some bottle material required pretreatment and different plastics need different solutions.

32
00:02:56,878 --> 00:03:01,520
The AI contribution was helping researchers find a better enzyme.

33
00:03:01,806 --> 00:03:09,828
Collecting, sorting, and industrial processing help determine whether a laboratory result becomes practical recycling.

34
00:03:10,094 --> 00:03:15,108
The next example uses AI to explore entirely new molecules.

35
00:03:16,142 --> 00:03:21,604
As bacteria develop resistance, researchers need more options for fighting infections.

36
00:03:22,222 --> 00:03:28,100
AI can help explore the enormous number of chemical structures that might have antibacterial properties.

37
00:03:28,430 --> 00:03:35,044
In one MIT-led study, generative models proposed more than 36 million possible molecules.

38
00:03:35,310 --> 00:03:38,660
Other models helped researchers prioritize promising candidates.

39
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:43,620
Then came the difficult part, making the molecules and finding out whether they worked.

40
00:03:43,918 --> 00:03:51,332
The team synthesized 24 compounds, seven showed selective antibacterial activity in laboratory tests.

41
00:03:51,758 --> 00:03:57,280
Two leads went further, and one reduced bacterial burden in a mouse model of gonorrhea.

42
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:00,324
Another did so in a mouse model of a MRSA skin infection.

43
00:04:00,654 --> 00:04:08,772
Those remain early candidates, they need further development, safety testing, and clinical trials before they can become medicines, but look at the change in the search process.

44
00:04:09,166 --> 00:04:17,188
Researchers could explore new chemical designs on a computer, narrow down the possibilities, and spend their experimental effort on a manageable set.

45
00:04:17,454 --> 00:04:19,360
AI helped expand the search.

46
00:04:19,662 --> 00:04:23,680
Chemistry and biology determined which ideas deserve to move forward.

47
00:04:23,950 --> 00:04:27,204
And sometimes the problem is interpreting biology we already have.

48
00:04:28,078 --> 00:04:32,080
Reading someone's DNA produces an enormous amount of information.

49
00:04:32,398 --> 00:04:35,760
Working out which changes might explain a disease is a different challenge.

50
00:04:36,078 --> 00:04:44,804
Google DeepMind's alpha genome predicts how changes in DNA could affect processes such as gene activity and RNA splicing.

51
00:04:45,198 --> 00:04:51,076
Splicing is the step where cells cut and join RNA instructions before using them to make proteins.

52
00:04:51,374 --> 00:04:53,348
A mistake can change the resulting protein.

53
00:04:53,582 --> 00:05:04,068
Researchers at the Broad Institute, working with the Greger Consortium, used a combined ranking system incorporating alpha genome to investigate unresolved rare diseases.

54
00:05:04,366 --> 00:05:11,920
In one child with severe epilepsy, it highlighted an overlooked change affecting a gene called DNM1.

55
00:05:12,366 --> 00:05:22,084
The model predicted a splicing problem in an RNA form mainly used in the brain, which earlier blood-based RNA tests hadn't clearly captured.

56
00:05:22,350 --> 00:05:25,348
Laboratory experiments supported that explanation.

57
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:31,140
This patient-associated analysis is preliminary research reported in a pre-print.

58
00:05:31,502 --> 00:05:33,668
It helped investigate a possible cause.

59
00:05:33,966 --> 00:05:36,388
A treatment still requires further work.

60
00:05:36,622 --> 00:05:43,364
That is a valuable role for AI, narrowing a huge search and suggesting a mechanism scientists can test.

61
00:05:43,758 --> 00:05:48,720
Understanding DNA also connects to improving our tools for changing it.

62
00:05:49,230 --> 00:05:53,508
Scientists used CRISPR tools to make targeted changes to DNA.

63
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:58,532
Those tools depend on molecular machinery adapted from microorganisms.

64
00:05:58,862 --> 00:06:02,116
The available proteins have different strengths and limitations.

65
00:06:02,510 --> 00:06:05,636
so researchers are continually looking for better ones.

66
00:06:05,966 --> 00:06:11,940
A company called ProFluent used protein language models to generate new versions of that machinery.

67
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:15,204
Those models learn patterns from biological sequences.

68
00:06:15,598 --> 00:06:20,932
Researchers then can use them to propose proteins with combinations of features worth testing.

69
00:06:21,198 --> 00:06:28,516
One results with OpenCRISPR1, an AI-designed gene editing protein that worked in human cells in the laboratory.

70
00:06:28,814 --> 00:06:31,428
That makes the contribution especially interesting.

71
00:06:32,142 --> 00:06:36,452
AI helped design a scientific tool that researchers can use to change biology.

72
00:06:36,686 --> 00:06:41,444
The longer-term possibility is a broader range of editors suited to different tasks.

73
00:06:41,934 --> 00:06:49,572
Each successful design gives researchers another starting point for developing the properties a particular application needs.

74
00:06:49,966 --> 00:06:52,960
Showing that an editor works in cells is an early step.

75
00:06:53,262 --> 00:07:00,644
Getting it into the right tissues, understanding unwanted effects, and demonstrating patient benefit require much more evidence.

76
00:07:01,134 --> 00:07:07,012
Still, the experiment shows that AI can help expand the toolkit available for future biotech.

77
00:07:07,214 --> 00:07:10,884
It can also help improve machinery plants already depend on.

78
00:07:11,726 --> 00:07:18,564
Plants use photosynthesis to turn sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide into material they can grow with.

79
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:27,940
As you probably learned in third grade, an important part of that process depends on an enzyme called rubisco, which helps capture carbon dioxide.

80
00:07:28,160 --> 00:07:31,748
Researchers have spent years trying to improve its performance.

81
00:07:32,110 --> 00:07:42,980
A team from MIT Stanford and the Australian National University used a machine learning model to identify promising parts of tobacco rubisco to investigate.

82
00:07:43,246 --> 00:07:48,164
They combined that guidance with laboratory evolution and experimental measurements.

83
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:54,052
In enzyme tests, one variant fixed carbon dioxide 29% faster than the original.

84
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:59,652
Improved versions could also assemble in plant chloroplasts where photosynthesis takes place.

85
00:08:00,014 --> 00:08:06,244
That creates an intriguing possibility for improving crops, but the measured result was better enzyme performance.

86
00:08:06,574 --> 00:08:13,572
Higher harvest yields still need to be demonstrated because plant growth depends on many interacting processes.

87
00:08:14,222 --> 00:08:19,204
AI helped focus the search for useful changes in a complex biological machine.

88
00:08:19,472 --> 00:08:27,492
This is an example of progress that could matter far beyond a laboratory, potentially helping plants use their resources more effectively.

89
00:08:27,792 --> 00:08:33,472
And the final use case could help scientists make progress on all the others.

90
00:08:33,998 --> 00:08:37,860
AI can propose something that looks promising and still be wrong.

91
00:08:38,112 --> 00:08:42,852
Experimental feedback gives researchers a way to improve the next attempt.

92
00:08:43,214 --> 00:08:50,564
Google research explored this with aptamers, short DNA molecules that can fold into shapes that bind proteins.

93
00:08:51,342 --> 00:08:57,988
AI agents proposed candidates, scientists tested them, and the measurements informed the next round.

94
00:08:58,286 --> 00:09:00,900
One experiment took a revealing wrong turn.

95
00:09:01,390 --> 00:09:06,468
The initial screening results looked more promising, but many candidates were simply sticky.

96
00:09:06,766 --> 00:09:11,172
They attached to other proteins and even to the magnetic beads used in the test.

97
00:09:11,534 --> 00:09:15,232
The agents analyzed those results and revised their approach.

98
00:09:15,438 --> 00:09:17,924
Later designs recovered more selective binding.

99
00:09:18,222 --> 00:09:19,396
That is the interesting loop.

100
00:09:19,726 --> 00:09:25,700
Propose an idea, test it in the physical world, investigate a misleading result, and try again.

101
00:09:25,998 --> 00:09:29,732
The work remains an early human supervised research prototype.

102
00:09:30,030 --> 00:09:38,884
In separate work, Google has also demonstrated infrastructure for agents to operate laboratory robots using dye experiments.

103
00:09:39,566 --> 00:09:43,140
Integrating those capabilities with molecular discovery is a next step.

104
00:09:43,374 --> 00:09:52,112
The future possibility is a tighter connection between scientific reasoning and physical experiments, with measurements continually improving the next decision.

105
00:09:52,334 --> 00:09:55,620
Kind of like RSI, except for biological research.

106
00:09:56,526 --> 00:10:03,940
Across these seven examples, AI is helping scientists understand biological information, propose useful designs, and choose what to test.

107
00:10:04,334 --> 00:10:09,632
Some of the results are molecules that work in laboratory experiments, others have reached animal studies.

108
00:10:09,934 --> 00:10:23,392
Turning them into reliable treatments, productive crops, or industrial processes takes much further evidence and engineering, but what we've discussed today is a very strong start and it's only expected to accelerate.

109
00:10:23,566 --> 00:10:27,844
The possibility I find most exciting is how these capabilities could reinforce each other.

110
00:10:28,110 --> 00:10:30,436
Better predictions suggest better experiments.

111
00:10:30,798 --> 00:10:36,452
Experiments reveal what the models missed, and those results can guide the next round of discovery.

112
00:10:36,782 --> 00:10:40,996
We are beginning to see that process work on real biological problems.

113
00:10:41,358 --> 00:10:45,988
How far it can scale is one of the most interesting questions about AI's future.

114
00:10:46,192 --> 00:10:51,620
Which of these seven applications was the most interesting, and how far do you think AI can take biology?

115
00:10:51,854 --> 00:10:52,832
Let me know down in the comments.

116
00:10:52,912 --> 00:10:55,312
If you like content just like this, please subscribe.

117
